{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe epic's last criterion is a live check of the fix-version sync on the production bridge. Every earlier child is pinned by tests over recorded or documented payloads only. Project REB has no Jira versions (read-only checks 2026-11-10 and 2026-11-11 PST), and the bridge never creates one (ADR 0124 decision 8). The operator ruled (2026-11-11) that this check runs through rebar itself: a probe ticket created in rebar syncs to REB, and its `releases` are set and changed through rebar. With REB holding no versions, the live path that rebar provides is the missing-version path (child gymnasial-snappy-lorikeet): a rebar release is withheld from Jira, kept on the ticket, alerted, retried each pass, and the alert resolves when the release no longer needs writing. Writing a version into Jira, and the Jira→rebar direction, need a version that exists in REB; those stay pinned by the recorded-payload tests of the inbound and outbound children, and their live check is split into a follow-up task that starts once REB has a people-created version.\n\n## Approach\n\n1. **Deploy.** The production bridge is `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml` (hourly at :23 on `main`, `pip install .` from the checkout, plus manual dispatch). Record the first run whose head SHA contains b878c4be768 (all code children).\n2. **Probe ticket.** Create a task through the rebar MCP `create_ticket` (or the worktree CLI) with `bridge_project=\"REB\"`, title `[probe] fix-version sync check (plucky-jetblack-parrot) - safe to close`, and a description saying it is a test artifact. After the next completed run it is bound to a new REB issue: read the key from `bindings[<probe id>].jira_key` in `.bridge_state/bindings.json` on the `tickets` branch (`git show origin/tickets:.bridge_state/bindings.json`), confirm it with the run log's outbound create line, and record it with the run id; `rebar show` confirms `bridge_project: REB`.\n3. **rebar→Jira, missing version.** `edit_ticket(probe, add_releases=[\"rebar-fixver-probe\"])` through MCP. After the next completed run: `rebar show` lists `releases: [\"rebar-fixver-probe\"]` and one unresolved `BRIDGE_ALERT` with reason `release-version-missing:rebar-fixver-probe`; a read-only `GET /rest/api/3/issue/<KEY>?fields=fixVersions` returns `[]`; the run log has no `fix version add failed` line for the key.\n4. **Steady state and retry.** The following completed run writes no new event on the probe (event count from the tracker before and after) and no `fixVersions` write for the key, and the alert stays unresolved.\n5. **Resolution.** `edit_ticket(probe, remove_releases=[\"rebar-fixver-probe\"])`. After the next completed run the alert is resolved and the Jira issue's `fixVersions` is still `[]`.\n6. **Follow-up.** Create a task, linked `discovered_from` this ticket, for the live checks that need an existing REB version (Jira→rebar sync, a rebar release written to Jira), to run once a person creates one.\n7. **Record and close.** One comment with run ids, PST times and outputs; close the probe ticket as obsolete with reason \"probe complete\" so its REB issue follows.\n\n## Scope\n\nNo repository files change: this is a live production check. Evidence lives in comments on this ticket and the epic.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] [non-codebase] The probe ticket created in rebar is bound to a REB issue after a completed bridge run on a `main` commit containing b878c4be768.\n  provenance: environment=production reconcile-bridge.yml on main; principal=bridge Jira account; privilege_posture=production-equivalent; instrument=live-call — run id, head SHA and the REB key from `.bridge_state/bindings.json` on the tickets branch\n- [ ] [non-codebase] A rebar release naming a version REB lacks is withheld: after the next run the ticket keeps it, carries one unresolved `release-version-missing:<name>` alert, and the Jira issue's `fixVersions` is `[]`.\n  provenance: environment=production bridge and Jira Cloud REB; principal=bridge account (writes), configured Jira identity (read-only GET); privilege_posture=production-equivalent; instrument=live-call — run id, `rebar show` and REST excerpts\n- [ ] [non-codebase] The following run writes no event on the probe and no `fixVersions` change, and the alert stays unresolved.\n  provenance: environment=production bridge; principal=bridge account; privilege_posture=production-equivalent; instrument=live-call — run id, tracker event counts, run log\n- [ ] [non-codebase] Removing the release in rebar resolves the alert after the next run.\n  provenance: environment=production bridge; principal=bridge account; privilege_posture=production-equivalent; instrument=live-call — run id and `rebar show` excerpt\n- [ ] A follow-up task for the version-dependent live checks exists, linked `discovered_from` this ticket.\n"}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1794441732326405001,"uuid":"4e582ada-33ce-4782-a9f0-ddf3005f821d"}